  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 115 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThe shortwave will be slow to exit the region and troughing over the \nRockies will pull the moisture axis north. This will result in \ncontinued rain chances moving into northern Iowa through the day \nSaturday. Light showers will linger in southern Iowa through \nHalloween night as the moisture axis is pulled west. Amounts \nSaturday will give the highest amounts to northern Iowa.\n\nHeight rises will reside over Iowa on Sunday, and the advection wing \nwill have lifted into Minnesota. This will keep Sunday cloudy and \ncooler, but dry. The complex synoptic trough will increase southwest \nflow Sunday night with rain beginning to arrive. Its influence will \nkeep precipitation chances going all the way through at least \nWednesday. The highest chance for thunderstorms looks to be Monday \nafternoon and evening. This is when the lower and upper level jet \nalign parallel to the cold front and the instability axis makes a \nrun north. Severe weather is questionable at this range given \nthe overnight and morning storms; cloudcover from these will \nmake surface destabilization challenging. Beyond Monday, a \nnorthern stream wave will usher in another cold front from the \nnorthwest, reinforcing the wrap around precip from the first \nsystem. Widespread moderate rain is possible from the cumulative\nrounds of rain. Most of the state could see a total between 1 \nto 2 inches, but the rainfall will be spread in a large enough \ntimespan that hydrology impacts will be minimal. A cooldown \nfollows the complex of waves with high temperatures returning \nto the 50s by next weekend.",
